    <notes>Seikilos
The Seikilos epitaph: the oldest surviving complete musical composition.
Source: M. L. West, Ancient Greek Music (Oxford 1992), no. 15, p. 301, in the
transcription a semitone above the stone's pitch (tonic f#). The stele is
Copenhagen, National Museum inv. 14897, 1st c. AD.

Hoson zes, phainou, / meden holos sy lypou; /
pros oligon esti to zen, / to telos ho chronos apaitei.
"While you live, shine; have no grief at all; life exists only for a short
while, and time demands its due."

The stone gives pitch and rhythm only. Our realisation choices: the tempo (a
dotted quarter at 66; the song tempo counts quarters, so 99), the octave
doubling and the drone on the lyre, and the aulos (double reed-pipe, here an
oboe) carrying the sung line in place of the voice: two pipes, the second a
few cents sharp and a few milliseconds late so they beat, a low drone pipe on
the tonic, a frame drum (tympanon) marking the two pulses of each bar, small cymbals
(kymbala) at the start of each line and on the final chord, and a
two-bar anabole (the kitharode's "striking up"): the lyre runs through its open
strings and settles on the fifth, the drones enter, and the singing begins at bar 3. A two-bar outro echoes
the final cadence on the lyre and settles on the tonic frame while the voice
holds its last note, then stops. The tempo broadens over the outro from 99
to 80 (tempo changes in the arrangement). The two aulos tracks stand in for the singer;
the reed drone is a second player.

The scale is kitmum, the Mixolydian mode on C of the Pythagorean gamut: C D E F G A B-flat = 1/1
9/8 81/64 4/3 3/2 27/16 16/9. In 53-EDO's notation the plain names are the
just major scale, so the Pythagorean E, A and B-flat are written E-up, A-up
and B-flat-down.</notes>
